        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I do agree that `-M` option is problematic in general sense. It would have been better if it allowed modifications to non-file buffers (like scratch buffers, etc.). But it seems to just set the global value of `'modifiable'` option to `false`. Good thing that this can be reverted.\n\nFor this type of \"plugin owned scratch buffers\" in 'mini.nvim' overall it might be a good idea to handle `'modifiable'` explicitly. Like setting them as non-modifiable while explicitly enabling the option temporarily when setting lines. The downside is that it will lead to a non-insignificant increase in code. The upside is that it will cover not only `-M` case, but the `set modifiable=false` case in general. Not sure yet if it is worth it though.\n\nBut what's for certain, there at least shouldn't be an infinite loop due to `modifiable=false`. Preferably only an error.\n\nI second the question, @macintacos. Would mind please sharing how this issue came up in real life? Are there good use cases for starting like `nvim -M`?",
