        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Definitely a fair question.\n\nI've been building some automations that essentially create new tmux tabs with pre-set pane layouts, where there's a file open in neovim in the right pane that is meant to be a read-only \"view\" into a file that I'm just using as a reference, with a vertically split pane on the left where I'm doing \"real\" work. It's a bit contrived, I know, and I could easily just \"work around\" this by just... not editing the buffer, but I kinda wanted to remove all possibility of me accidentally editing the neovim instance that is running on the right that is explicitly being used as a reference.\n\nTotally understandable if y'all don't think it's worth addressing or anything like that. Just wanted to report the odd behavior because when I was doing this, I was doing a lot of tests and opening these layouts multiple times, and just saw my CPU go through the roof for what seemed like no apparent reason. ",
